  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on metropolitan areas within a single country, the Czech Republic, which may limit direct generalizability to countries with different land planning systems or suburban dynamics.",
      "what": "This study analyzed land transaction data across metropolitan areas in the Czech Republic using a Bayesian Vector Autoregression with exogenous variables (BVARX) model. The analysis reveals that the COVID-19 pandemic acted as a demand shock that accelerated suburbanization, with large metropolitan areas experiencing strong, persistent land market pressures that outlasted the pandemic, while mid-sized cities showed much more muted responses.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that pandemic-induced spatial shifts in land markets are not temporary disruptions but lasting structural transformations, and that a city's specific size and regional integration dictate how vulnerable its suburban fringe is to sudden demand shocks."
    }
